#!/usr/bin/env bash
# qwen21 - wrapper for Qwen-Image-2.1 generation on Apple MLX, via mflux
# (Python-MLX) through the PEP 723 script qwen21_gen.py. No harness to build and
# no Xcode: uv fetches mflux and its deps on first run, mflux fetches the weights.
# Every flag is forwarded to mflux's own parser; the script only fills in the
# defaults this repo wants (int8, 40 steps, 1024 square) when you omit them.
#
# Run `qwen21 help` for full usage (weights, licence, flags, examples).

set -eu

usage() {
    cat <<'EOF'
qwen21 - Qwen-Image-2.1 generation and editing on Apple Silicon

  qwen21 <flags>        text-to-image and image-to-image, via mflux (MLX)
  qwen21 edit <flags>   instruction editing, via diffusers (MPS)
  qwen21 server         a local web page, model resident, nothing saved

Wraps mflux's `mflux-generate-qwen-2.1` through the sibling PEP 723 script
qwen21_gen.py. uv resolves mflux on first run; mflux downloads the weights on
first generation. This wrapper downloads nothing itself and builds nothing.

MODEL
  Qwen-Image-2.1: a 7.1B single-stream, block-causal DiT with a Qwen3-VL text
  encoder and a 64-channel causal VAE (16x spatial compression). It is trained
  for guidance-free sampling, so the default is 40 steps at guidance 1.0.

WEIGHTS (automatic, one-time, NOT gated)
  Repo:    https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen-Image-2.1
  Licence: Qwen Research License Agreement. Research use only. This is stricter
           than the licences on the flux2 and krea2 backends - check it before
           you use any output commercially.
  Size:    ~33 GB bf16 on disk - 14.2 GB transformer, 17.5 GB text encoder,
           1.4 GB VAE. mflux puts them in the HuggingFace cache
           (~/.cache/huggingface), downloads them on the first generation, and
           needs no token.

MEMORY AND PRECISION
  The default is bf16. Quantization applies to the transformer and the VAE
  only; the 17.5 GB Qwen3-VL text encoder is never quantized and stays
  resident, so it sets the floor.

  Measured here on an M5 Pro at 1024x1024, 40 steps, six runs:

    peak memory   29.9 to 30.7 GB at BOTH bf16 and -q 8, never higher at bf16
    speed         3.3 s/step cold, 4.3 to 4.6 s/step warm, at BOTH precisions

  So on a 64 GB machine -q 8 buys no measurable memory and no measurable
  speed, and it costs accuracy. That is why bf16 is the default. Pass -q 8
  when another process needs the RAM.

  Upstream measured ~46 GB peak at bf16 and ~30.7 GB at -q 8 on an M5 Max.
  Our runs do not reproduce that gap. One hypothesis, unproven: mflux loads
  the bf16 weights and quantizes them in memory, so the peak carries the
  unquantized originals either way. Trust each run's own "Peak MLX memory"
  line over any table, including this one.

  mflux itself accepts only -q 3/4/5/6/8 and reaches bf16 by the flag being
  absent. qwen21_gen.py takes `-q 0` as a synonym for that, so one flag spans
  every precision. --low-ram and --vae-tiling cut peak memory further at a
  speed cost.

RESOLUTION IS THE REAL SPEED DIAL
  It moves time and memory far more than precision does. Measured here on an
  M5 Pro, bf16, 10 steps, warm cache, one run each:

    size          s/step   10 steps   peak memory   latent tokens
    512 x 512      1.01      10.1s      20.5 GB        1024
    768 x 768      1.79      20.3s      25.1 GB        2304
    1024 x 1024    3.32      41.2s      30.7 GB        4096
    1328 x 1328    6.14      70.8s      39.8 GB        6889

  Time tracks the token count almost linearly, so it is the pixel count that
  costs, not attention blowing up. 512 runs 3.3x faster than 1024 and saves
  10 GB. At the 40-step default that is about 40s against 133s.

  The catch: this model is trained around 1 MP. A simple subject at 512 looks
  fine. Text in the image is a known strength of this model and it is NOT
  tested at 512 here, so check it yourself before you trust small sizes for
  anything with words in it.

  Warm the cache before you trust any timing. The same 512 run measured 30.1s
  cold and 10.1s warm, and an earlier sweep read 97.2s at 1024 while the
  machine was swapping. Take each run's own numbers over any table, this one
  included.

DO NOT RUN TWO AT ONCE
  Two 512 generations, same prompt and steps, 20.5 GB each, so both fit in
  64 GB without swapping:

    one after the other    11.0s + 11.1s     26s wall clock
    both at the same time  27.5s + 27.7s     31s wall clock

  Each run slows 2.5x when they overlap, and the overlap does not win it back.
  One generation already saturates the GPU. `qwen21 server` queues them for
  this reason.

DEFAULTS INJECTED (omit the flag to get these; pass it to override)
  (no -q flag)      bf16 transformer and VAE
  --steps 40        upstream's recommended guidance-free default
  --width/--height  1024 square, text-to-image only (i2i takes the input's size)
  --output          qwen21.png in your cwd
  --auto-seeds 1    random seed, printed so you can reproduce it

ENV
  No wrapper-specific variables. mflux honours the usual HuggingFace ones, so
  HF_HOME relocates the weight cache.

TEXT IN THE IMAGE
  The model renders text well, but only text you spell out. Quote the exact
  string you want. A prompt that asks for "some explanatory notes" or "a short
  caption" gives you text-shaped texture with no real characters, in Latin and
  CJK alike.

  Measured here, Traditional Chinese at int8, 1024x1024:
    simple sign, 13 characters, 2 strings       13/13 correct
    dense catalog page, 41 characters, 9 strings  37/41 correct
    same page with the note text left unspecified  every note was fake glyphs

  The remaining errors land on high-stroke characters and are not size-related: the
  same word rendered correctly in 12px body copy and wrong in a 40px heading
  on one page. Re-roll the seed or simplify the layout rather than enlarging.

EDITING (qwen21 edit ...)
  `qwen21 edit` is a second engine, diffusers on MPS, not mflux. mflux has not
  ported the Qwen3-VL vision tower, so it cannot do instruction editing at all.
  diffusers loads the full VL model, vision tower included, which is what lets
  the transformer act on a reference image.

    qwen21 edit --prompt "make it night, lights on" --image room.png

  It takes up to 10 references (repeat --image), supports local edits driven by
  circles or painted annotations on the reference, and reuses the same cached
  weights. It is bf16 only: there is no int8 path here, and the vision tower is
  resident on top of the 33 GB of weights, so it needs more memory than the
  mflux side. Pass --offload when it will not fit.

  Run `qwen21 edit --help` for its flags.

  MPS VAE ENCODE, PATCHED HERE
  The MPS backend miscomputes the VAE encoder. Every reference image lost 73%
  of its contrast, and the tonal range collapsed into a mid-grey band. A VAE
  encode/decode round trip with no transformer reproduced the defect, so a
  device sweep on one image localised it:

                        mean   contrast   tonal range (p1-p99)
    source             147.6      64.2     4.7 - 230.3
    mps bf16           100.4      17.8    53.0 - 144.7
    mps fp32           100.5      17.8    53.0 - 144.7
    cpu fp32           147.8      64.2     5.3 - 230.3

  Both MPS dtypes fail. CPU reproduces the source. So this is a device bug,
  not a precision bug, which is why fp32 alone did not help. Text-to-image
  never hits it because that path only decodes.

  The fix runs the VAE encode on CPU in fp32 and keeps the 40 denoise steps on
  MPS. It is the default. Encode runs once per reference image, so the cost is
  about 16s on a 1024 edit: 202s against 186s. Peak memory does not change.
  Pass --vae-encode-on-mps to retest the broken path after a PyTorch upgrade.

  Verified on a full edit, seed 505, one sign rewritten: contrast 62.0 against
  the source image's 63.7, tonal range 6 - 232 against 4 - 232.

  Root cause is not proven, and nothing is filed upstream. macOS 26.6.2 here
  is below the build that fixed a near-identical PyTorch MPS report, so an OS
  update may remove the whole problem. Full write-up, evidence and next steps:
  qwen21/MPS-VAE-ENCODE.md, with the probe in qwen21/vae_device_sweep.py.

  Separate upstream bug found on the way: `prepare_latents` casts the input
  image to the PIPELINE dtype, while the decode path casts to `vae.dtype`. So
  `pipe.vae.to(torch.float32)` alone raises a dtype error. The CPU encode shim
  moves the image and the VAE together, so it avoids that seam.

SERVER (qwen21 server)
  Serves a one-page web UI on http://127.0.0.1:8765.

  Use it for the form and for the privacy, not for speed. The model does stay
  resident, but that buys nothing measurable: mflux memory-maps the weights, so
  the same 8-step 1024 image took 30.2s through the CLI and 31s through the
  server. The win is setting the knobs in a browser instead of retyping flags.

  Nothing is logged and nothing is saved. The prompt never reaches disk, the
  image never reaches disk, and no history is kept after the response. mflux
  normally writes the prompt into the PNG as EXIF on save; this path never calls
  save, so there is no metadata either. HTTP access logging is off. Use the
  Download button in the page when you want to keep an image.

  It binds 127.0.0.1, so it is not reachable from the network. Pass --host to
  change that, and read the paragraph above again before you do.

    qwen21 server                      # http://127.0.0.1:8765
    qwen21 server --port 9000
    qwen21 server -q 8                 # int8 weights, for when RAM is tight

  MEMORY. The first version of this server crashed a 64 GB machine on the third
  image of a batch. Not a leak: memory is flat across generations. The cause was
  that it held the 17.5 GB Qwen3-VL text encoder through all 40 denoising steps.
  The CLI drops it once the prompt is encoded, through mflux's MemorySaver, and
  this server registered no callbacks at all. Measured at 1024:

                          peak      active     cache
    first version        45.01 GB  30.39 GB  22.42 GB
    now                  30.68 GB  15.25 GB   0.00 GB

  30.68 GB is exactly what the CLI reports for the same image. The encoder is
  gone after the first generation, so a new prompt rebuilds the model. That
  costs 1.0s against 40s for the generation. A batch reuses one prompt and pays
  nothing.

  PROGRESS AND CANCEL share one button. Idle it reads Generate or Edit. While a
  run is in flight it turns red and reads Cancel, and after you press it it
  reads Stopping. The panel shows a spinner, a bar with the step count, the
  position in the batch, and a running clock. The page polls /progress once a
  second; there is no websocket and no extra dependency.

  Cancel on a generate stops at the end of the current step, so the wait is one
  step: about 1s at 512 and about 3.5s at 1024. Cancel on an edit kills the
  child process group, which took under 2s. Either way the rest of the batch is
  dropped, and images already finished stay on the page.

  NO LIVE PREVIEW, on purpose. Showing the half-finished image means a full VAE
  decode per preview, and Qwen-Image-2.1 uses a 64-channel causal VAE at 16x
  compression. The decode alone timed 1.40s at 512, 7.42s at 768 and 8.05s at
  1024. Wall clock is worse still, because the decode's allocations disturb the
  steps that follow. At 1024, 40 steps, two runs each:

    no previews          157.4s  154.9s
    every 20 steps       187.1s  176.0s
    every 5 steps        248.3s  295.6s

  One preview at 1024 costs about 25s. The step counter is free, so that is what
  the page shows.

  EDIT MODE in the page runs qwen21_edit.py as a child process, because editing
  needs diffusers and the vision tower while the server runs on mflux. The job
  goes over pipes as JSON, so the reference images never become files and the
  prompt never appears in `ps`. The mflux model is dropped first: the edit engine
  measured 38.7 GiB allocated on MPS and the two do not fit together. The next
  generation rebuilds it in 3.8s.

  The child stays alive between edits, because loading its pipeline costs 20 to
  36s and nothing about it depends on the job. Two edits through the server took
  34.2s then 22.1s. Switching back to generating kills it, since the two engines
  cannot both be resident. Cancel kills it too, so the next edit reloads.

  WHAT AN EDIT COSTS. Seconds per denoising step, measured on this machine:

    megapixels   0.26   0.59   1.05   1.60   1.75
    mflux t2i    1.01   1.79   3.32      -   6.14
    edit         0.76   1.87   3.52   7.95   7.40

  The two engines cost about the same per step, which is the evidence that the
  edit path is really running on the GPU: PYTORCH_ENABLE_MPS_FALLBACK is on, and
  an op falling back to CPU would not land within 25% of a native MLX build.

  Cost climbs faster than pixel count, because attention is quadratic in token
  count. So the output size is the expensive dial, not the step count. The same
  edit, same prompt and seed, both correct:

    1265 px, 40 steps    345s
     768 px, 24 steps     72s

  The page prints an estimate under the button before you press it, from a fit
  of the table above. "Match the reference" on a large screenshot lands at the
  top of that curve, so read the estimate before you commit to it.

  Editing takes up to 10 references and defaults the output size to the
  reference's own, capped at 1328. output_resolution is an area budget, not a
  side: the pipeline calls calculate_dimensions(res * res, reference ratio), so
  the shape always follows the reference. A 1280x720 reference comes back
  1280x704, rounded to the multiple of 32 the pipeline requires.

  RGBA IS REAL on the edit path. The 2.1 VAE has 4 channels in and out, the
  pipeline converts every reference to RGBA, and the diffusers image processor
  leaves alpha alone. Asking an edit to cut the subject out returned an image
  with alpha spanning 0 to 255: 24% of pixels near transparent, 71% fully
  opaque. mflux drops the 4th channel on decode, so the generate path stays RGB.

  BATCHING is sequential and buys no speed. mflux has no batched inference:
  generate_image takes no batch dimension, and --auto-seeds only loops over N
  seeds. So N images cost N times one image. The Count field, up to 8, saves you
  from resubmitting the form and nothing more. Each image appears as soon as it
  is done rather than all at the end. With an explicit seed the batch walks
  seed, seed+1, seed+2, so any image in it is reproducible from the CLI.

  IMAGE-TO-IMAGE takes one reference image, with a strength dial. The reference
  is handed to mflux as a PIL object, not a file, so it never reaches disk. Pick
  "Match reference" for the size and the output keeps the reference's aspect
  ratio at about 1 MP; any fixed size resizes the reference to it, which squashes
  a photo that is not the same shape.

  Strength is the share of the denoising schedule that gets skipped, from
  init_time_step = max(1, int(steps * strength)). So 0.4 starts the run 40% of
  the way in. It never reaches zero, and a low value still keeps the reference's
  colour and composition. Measured on one pear at 8 steps, as RMS distance from
  the reference image:

    strength 0.15   17.1      strength 0.85    2.5
    strength 0.55    4.0      no reference    74.5

  The dial is monotonic and it works, but read the spread: every strength sits
  far closer to the reference than to the same prompt with no reference. Drop the
  reference entirely when you want the prompt to win.

  ONE reference, not several. The model handles up to 10, but only through the
  diffusers edit pipeline, and mflux's generate_image exposes a single
  image_path. `qwen21 edit` is the multi-reference path and stays on the CLI.

  Instruction editing also stays on the CLI, for the same reason.

    qwen21 server                 # http://127.0.0.1:8765
    qwen21 server --port 9000
    qwen21 server -q 8            # int8 weights, for when RAM is tight

  The page loads htmx from a CDN. That is the only outbound request, and it
  carries no prompt data.

STILL NOT SUPPORTED
  LoRA, on either engine. Transparent/RGBA output: the model has it, but the
  mflux VAE decodes RGB only.

FLAGS
  Forwarded verbatim to mflux. `qwen21 --help` lists every one from mflux's own
  parser, so the list never drifts. The ones you want first:
    --prompt            the prompt (required; or --prompt-file)
    --negative-prompt   enables true CFG; pair it with --guidance > 1
    --image PATH [STR]  reference image plus optional strength, switches to i2i
    --steps             denoising steps
    --seed              fixed seed; repeat the flag for a batch
    --auto-seeds N      N random seeds in one run, model loads once
    -q                  0 for bf16 (default), or 8, 6, 5, 4, 3 to quantize
    --low-ram           trade speed for a lower peak
    --vae-tiling        decode in tiles; cuts the decode-time spike
    --width / --height  output size
    --output            output path; {seed} in the name expands per seed

EXAMPLES
  # text-to-image at the defaults (int8, 40 steps, 1024 square)
  qwen21 --prompt "a beaver building a dam, photorealistic, golden hour"

  # fixed seed, explicit output
  qwen21 --prompt "a red apple" --seed 42 --output apple.png

  # image-to-image; size comes from the reference. The optional second value
  # after the path is the strength (default 0.4).
  qwen21 --prompt "make it winter" --image ref.png 0.6 --output winter.png

  # true CFG with a negative prompt
  qwen21 --prompt "a red apple" --negative-prompt "blurry, low quality" --guidance 2.5

  # batch of 4 random seeds, one model load
  qwen21 --prompt "a red apple" --auto-seeds 4 --output "apple_{seed}.png"

  # int8, for when another process needs the memory
  qwen21 --prompt "a red apple" -q 8
EOF
}

script_dir="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"

# `qwen21 help` prints this wrapper's usage. `--help` falls through to mflux's
# own parser, which is authoritative for the flag list.
case "${1:-}" in
    help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    edit) shift; exec uv run "$script_dir/qwen21_edit.py" "$@" ;;
    server) shift; exec uv run "$script_dir/qwen21_server.py" "$@" ;;
esac

exec uv run "$script_dir/qwen21_gen.py" "$@"
